The instruction says "One line that names the next step". This could be read as a count limit of one line, which would be a constraint of at most one line. It is a structural rule about the reply: the next step is to be named in one line. "One line" works as a maximum on the length of that part. The previous unit, "One or two lines of reason", is a similar structure, and there the number is a limit on lines.

I would give 1 line, le, constraint. The wording "One line" could also mean exactly one, which is also at most one, so le fits.

{"limits": [{"limit": 1, "comparison": "le", "role": "constraint", "unit": "lines naming the next step"}]}

The instruction is a list item under "Leave out the following in every message": apologies longer than one sentence. The limit is one sentence. Apologies of more than one sentence are left out, so the range where the rule applies is more than one sentence. The compliant range is at most one sentence.

The role is a judgement call. The rule prohibits apologies longer than one sentence, so the comparison that describes the allowed range is "le 1" as a constraint on apology length. The trigger reading would be "gt 1", the range where the rule applies. The rule is about what is allowed, so I treat the number as a constraint on apology length, which is at most 1 sentence.

{"limits": [{"limit": 1, "comparison": "le", "role": "constraint", "unit": "sentences in an apology"}]}

The instruction points at the units under REFERRED, so its numbers come from there. Those give two limits: three sentences at most for a chat message, and no more than eight lines for an email.

The instruction says to split the answer when it needs more than the limit allows. That makes each limit the trigger for the split, so the rule applies once the answer exceeds the limit. The comparison is therefore "gt" for both, with the role as trigger.

